  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 PM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nA relatively fast west/northwest upper flow across the Great Lakes \nover the next several days, with a trough digging over the Pacific \nNorthwest and high pressure anchored over the southern CONUS. \nThe main focus for precipitation in the near term sits along a \nfrontal boundary over the Ohio Valley. This frontal feature \nwill drift northward over the weekend, with precipitation encountering\nan increasingly more hostile atmosphere over northern Michigan,\nthanks to a dry, easterly low level flow. Chilly temperatures \non the way, as northern stream energy reinforces lower heights \nover the northern Great Lakes. \n\nModels continue to advertise a southward focus to the weekend rain \nchances, as the heaviest rain corridor stretches from portions of \nsouthern Wisconsin into southern Michigan.  There are still some \nquestion marks on the northern extent of the precipitation, with \nmodels trying to bring at least lighter rain toward the M-72 (or \neven M-32) corridor.  Although the deepest moisture/forcing will \nlikely be south of our area, we will see lighter precipitation \nedging further north Saturday afternoon and evening, as the \naforementioned frontal boundary drifts slowly northward. There \nwill be a tight gradient (thanks to the persistent dry low level\nair intrusion) between the heavier rain areas to the south and \nthe potential spottier, much lighter rain further north. As of \nright now, it looks like M-55 will be the dividing line between \nheavier rains to the south and much lighter rain to the north. \n\nA stretch of cooler temperatures upcoming into next week, as \nadditional northern stream energy helps to suppress upper heights \nwhile also dragging cooler H8 temperatures across the northern \nlakes. These cooler temperatures will also bring about chances of at \nleast patchy frost early next week.  Minimal rain chances next week \nwith cool, dry Canadian high pressure in control.",
